2026-09-23T12:47:38.4219746Z     The ``pytest`` framework makes it easy to write small tests, yet
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4220227Z     scales to support complex functional testing for applications and libraries.
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4220610Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4220804Z     An example of a simple test:
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4221040Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4221226Z     .. code-block:: python
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4221447Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4221643Z         # content of test_sample.py
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4221896Z         def inc(x):
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4222219Z             return x + 1
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4222433Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4222603Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4222779Z         def test_answer():
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4223009Z             assert inc(3) == 5
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4223241Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4223408Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4223587Z     To execute it::
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4223792Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4223969Z         $ pytest
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4224246Z         ============================= test session starts =============================
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4224593Z         collected 1 items
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4224809Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4224997Z         test_sample.py F
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4225210Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4225440Z         ================================== FAILURES ===================================
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4225851Z         _________________________________ test_answer _________________________________
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4226191Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4226373Z             def test_answer():
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4226611Z         >       assert inc(3) == 5
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4226861Z         E       assert 4 == 5
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4227101Z         E        +  where 4 = inc(3)
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4227341Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4227539Z         test_sample.py:5: AssertionError
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4228037Z         ========================== 1 failed in 0.04 seconds ===========================
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4228365Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4228537Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4229373Z     Thanks to ``pytest``'s detailed assertion introspection, you can simply use plain ``assert`` statements. See `getting-started <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html#our-first-test-run>`_ for more examples.
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4230276Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4230447Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4230619Z     Features
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4230813Z     --------
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4231102Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4231658Z     - Detailed info on failing `assert statements <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/assert.html>`_ (no need to remember ``self.assert*`` names)
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4232407Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4232589Z     - `Auto-discovery
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4233008Z       <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/goodpractices.html#python-test-discovery>`_
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4233486Z       of test modules and functions
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4233732Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4234087Z     - `Modular fixtures <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/fixtures.html>`_ for
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4234600Z       managing small or parametrized long-lived test resources
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4234918Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4235272Z     - Can run `unittest <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/unittest.html>`_ (or trial)
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4235719Z       test suites out of the box
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4235958Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4236143Z     - Python 3.10+ or PyPy3
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4236366Z     
2026-09-23T12:47:38.4236930Z     - Rich plugin architecture, with over 1300+ `external plugins <https://docs.pytest.org/en/latest/reference/plugin_list.html>`_ and thriving community
